{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"content_hash":"c1fb6ba6e10cf32408b2b10111707435607464f831f5e00e125267521e61a833","digest":{"component_or_area":"event store index lock test","key_entities":["test_contended_index_lock_cleared_during_backoff_succeeds","src/rebar/_store/event_append.py","gitutil._with_index_lock_retry","_retry_probe","threading.Timer","_reclaim_if_stale_index_lock","index.lock"],"problem_keywords":["test flaky","timing race","index lock","macOS","py3.13","git commit","lock management"],"propositions":["A wall-clock timer in the test deletes git's real index.lock mid-commit on slow runners, causing fatal write failure.","The test plants a young index.lock and arms a 0.4s timer to release it, but on macOS the timer fires while git still holds its own lock.","force_reclaim=True causes the planted lock to be removed on first retry, so git.commit creates a new real index.lock that the timer then deletes.","The timer lambda has no identity check to distinguish the test's planted lock from git's real lock, making it unsafe on slow machines.","Replacing the wall-clock timer with gitutil._retry_probe would release the lock deterministically after observed failures rather than elapsed time."]},"digest_hash_version":1,"model":"claude-opus-4-8","schema":"ticket_digest_v1"},"env_id":"b2b5c498-2f67-4ae3-bafc-eca40294bec2","event_type":"TICKET_DIGEST","timestamp":1787582727595580529,"uuid":"e5cdc344-740a-4f5d-8116-0e6c2f3a1584"}